"In the first verse of Surah al-Qamar, it is stated that the Hour of Resurrection has drawn near and the moon has split. This is one of the greatest miracles of the Prophet (peace be upon him). By the consensus of the commentators (mufassirun), this verse reports the event of the splitting of the moon, which took place in Mecca upon the polytheists' demand for a miracle. The event was narrated by many Companions.\n\nIn hadith sources such as al-Bukhari, Muslim, and al-Tirmidhi, narrations from Companions such as Ibn Mas'ud, Ibn Abbas, Anas, and Hudhayfah report that the moon split into two parts, one part being seen in front of the mountain and the other behind it, and that the Messenger of Allah said, \"Bear witness!\" The polytheists described this as sorcery; however, when those arriving from other regions also stated that they had seen it in the same manner, they were astounded.\n\nIn some narrations, the expression \"twice\" (marratayn) appears. While some scholars interpreted this as two separate splittings, the majority stated that it means \"two parts\" (firqatayn), and that the splitting occurred once but opened and closed swiftly. Hafiz Ibn Hajar states that he knows of no hadith scholar who said there was more than one splitting.\n\nIt has been narrated that some scholars, such as al-Hasan al-Basri and 'Ata', said, \"The moon will split on the Day of Resurrection.\" However, this does not mean that they denied the miracle; rather, they intended to emphasize that the past splitting was a sign of the great splitting on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed, the continuation of the verse reports that whenever they see a miracle, they turn away and call it \"sorcery,\" which demonstrates that the event occurred in the past.\n\nAmong the philosophers, Ibn Sina argued that the splitting of celestial bodies was impossible, yet this view of his was based on Aristotelian physics. Today, it is scientifically accepted that the moon can break apart. Miracles occur through the power of Allah and are rationally possible. The Quran's reporting of this event is the strongest evidence of its truthfulness.\n\nMuhyiddin Ibn Arabi compares the splitting of the moon to the emergence of the perfect human (al-insan al-kamil). According to him, the moon is a celestial body that changes between crescent and full moon, and its splitting points to the manifestation of divine commands. This event witnessed by the Companions is a physical miracle proving the truth of the Prophet.\n\nIn conclusion, the miracle of the splitting of the moon is established through mutawatir (mass-transmitted) reports and accepted by the vast majority of commentators. Metaphorical interpretations, on the other hand, contradict the context (siyaq) of the verse and authentic narrations. This miracle demonstrates that the Day of Resurrection has drawn near and that Allah has power over all things.\n\nThe verse mentions the miracle of the splitting of the moon at the beginning of Surah al-Qamar. When the polytheists saw this miracle, they said, \"continuous sorcery.\" They would only believe in what they clearly saw. Even though the miracle took place, they still did not believe and characterized it as sorcery. The Prophet's duty is to display miracles, not to compel belief. When they saw the moon split, they asked travelers coming from other places, and when those travelers also stated that they had seen it, they said, \"this is continuous sorcery.\"\n\nMuhyiddin Ibn Arabi mentioned the possibility that the splitting of the moon took place in the eye of the beholder. However, this does not mean that he denied the miracle. He merely described the perspective of the polytheists. Ismail Fenni Ertugrul criticizes this interpretation, stating that such an explanation would reduce the miracle to the level of sorcery. He believes that the splitting of the moon truly took place and notes that it is established by mutawatir hadiths. The majority of commentators are also of this view.\n\nIn the verses that follow, it is explained that the polytheists rejected the Prophet and what he brought, and followed their desires. Every matter has an appointed goal and is determined with Allah. News of past nations and warnings regarding the Hereafter came to them. These warnings are profound wisdom, yet they still did not believe. The Prophet is commanded to turn away from them and await the Day of Resurrection. On that Day, the caller (Israfil) will call to a terrifying thing, and people will emerge from their graves like swarming locusts. The disbelievers will say that this is a very difficult day.\n\nThe people of Noah also denied their prophet and called him \"mad.\" When Noah was overcome and prayed to Allah, Allah opened the gates of heaven with pouring water, caused the earth to burst forth with springs, and the waters met. As for Noah, He carried him on an ark made of planks and nails, which sailed under Allah's watchful eyes. This was done as a reward for Noah. Allah left the ark as a sign (ayah). Yet how few people reflect and take heed?\n\nThe people of 'Ad also denied the warnings. Allah sent against them a furious, roaring wind (sarsar) on a day of continuous ill fortune. This wind plucked people away as if they were uprooted trunks of palm trees. How terrible were Allah's punishment and warnings! The Quran has been made easy to understand, yet there are so few who take heed.\n\nThe people of Thamud also denied the warnings and said, \"Shall we follow a human being from among ourselves?\" They envied that revelation was given to Salih instead of one of them, and accused him of lying. Allah announced that He would send a female camel (naqah) as a trial. This she-camel was to be a miracle for them. However, they were warned not to harm the camel. Thus, the destruction of each nation came about on account of their own disbelief.\n\nThe she-camel sent to the people of Thamud was released as a trial. Here, the word \"fitnah\" means test or trial. According to al-Razi, the primary purpose was to confirm the prophet; for the she-camel was a miracle by being brought forth from a rock, and a trial by being released to roam among them and by the division of water. Through these two aspects, it was a test distinguishing between those who would earn reward and those who would incur punishment.\n\nThe water was apportioned in turns between the she-camel and the people. One day the she-camel would drink, and one day they would take water. The people would draw water on their turn, and on the she-camel's turn, they would benefit from her milk. This she-camel was a magnificent creature that brought both a burden and a blessing; being called \"the camel of Allah,\" it was managed like an endowed property (waqf). However, this apportionment and burden became too heavy for them, and they could not endure it.\n\nQudar ibn Salif, one of the notables of the people, hamstrung the she-camel and killed it. Salih (peace be upon him) warned them of punishment within three days: their faces would turn yellow, then red, then black, and on the fourth day, punishment would befall them. When they saw the signs, they sought to kill Salih, but he was saved along with the believers. On the fourth day, they were destroyed by a violent blast and earthquake; they became like the trampled, dry twigs of an enclosure builder.\n\nThe people of Lot also denied the warnings. We sent against them a wind showering stones; Lot and his believing family were saved, except for his wife. Lot had warned them of the terror of the punishment, but they sought to do evil to his guests (the angels). Thereupon, We obliterated their sight and said, \"Taste My punishment and My warnings!\" Early in the morning, an abiding punishment seized them.\n\nWarnings also came to the people of Pharaoh, but they rejected all Our signs. So We seized them with the seizure of One Mighty and Powerful. Are your disbelievers now better than those? Or do you have an exemption written in the scriptures? Or do they say, \"We are a victorious assembly\"? That assembly will be defeated, and they will turn their backs and flee. This is a miracle that took place on the day of Badr. Yet the Hour of Resurrection is their appointed time, and that Hour is far more catastrophic and bitter.\n\nThe criminals are in error and madness. On that Day, they will be dragged on their faces into the Fire, and it will be said, 'Taste the touch of Hell!' We have created everything according to a destiny. Our command is but one, like the blinking of an eye. Indeed, We destroyed your likes; is there anyone who will take heed? Everything that is done is present in the books; small and great, all is written down. The righteous are in gardens, in light, and on seats of truth in the presence of an all-powerful Sovereign."